The code statements that lead to the bug are:
flush_workqueue(psmouse->wq) in psmouse_disconnect();
psmouse_queue_work(psmouse, &priv->dev3_register_work, ...) in alps_report_bare_ps2_packet();
kfree(priv) in alps_disconnect();
Reason:
`flush_workqueue()` only waits for work items already queued before its call. After it returns, a new `dev3_register_work` can be scheduled by `psmouse_queue_work()` from an incoming PS/2 packet. Then `alps_disconnect()` frees the `priv` structure. When the delayed work later runs, it accesses the freed `priv` and its `dev3` field, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
disable_delayed_work_sync(&priv->dev3_register_work);
kfree(priv);
Reason:
`disable_delayed_work_sync()` cancels the delayed work and waits for its completion, ensuring it cannot execute after the deallocation. By placing it before `kfree(priv)`, the `priv` structure remains valid until the work is fully stopped, preventing the use-after-free.

